\section{Correction Prescribed by the Identity}
\label{sec:method}

Theorem~\ref{thm:bias_identity} identifies per-subgroup misclassification deviation as the bias source; the natural correction is per-subgroup confusion matrix inversion.
This correction (EC-HTE) proceeds in three stages: estimate per-subgroup confusion matrices via hierarchical Bayes, invert them to correct observed outcome moments, and propagate correction uncertainty into downstream causal estimators.
The complete procedure is summarized in Algorithm~\ref{alg:echte} and illustrated in Figure~\ref{fig:method_overview}.

\begin{algorithm}[t]
\caption{EC-HTE: Error-Corrected HTE}
\label{alg:echte}
\begin{algorithmic}[1]
\Require $\{(Y_i, W_i, \hat{Z}_i, X_i)\}_{i=1}^N$; expert labels $\{Z_j^*\}_{j=1}^{n_{\text{expert}}}$; subgroup map $S$
\Ensure Corrected CATE estimates $\hat{\tau}(z)$ with CIs
\Statex
\State \textbf{Check:} $n_{\text{expert}} \cdot P(Z_{\min}) / K \geq 25$ and $\max_s \pi_s < 0.5$
\Statex
\State \textbf{Step 1: Per-subgroup CMs (hierarchical Bayes)}
\State $\alpha_0 \gets \arg\max_\alpha \prod_s p(\text{data}_s \mid \alpha, \hat{C}_{\text{global}})$
\For{$s = 1, \ldots, K$}
    \State $\hat{C}_s[j,k] \gets (n_{s,jk} + \alpha_0 \hat{C}_{\text{global},j,k}) / (n_{s,j\cdot} + \alpha_0)$
\EndFor
\State \textbf{Step 2: Stratified inversion}
\For{$s = 1, \ldots, K$}
    \If{$\kappa(\hat{C}_s) > 100$} $\hat{C}_s \gets \bar{C}$ \Comment{$\kappa$-guard}
    \EndIf
    \State $\tilde{\boldsymbol{\mu}}_s \gets \hat{C}_s^{-1} \hat{\boldsymbol{\mu}}_s$
\EndFor
\State \textbf{Step 3: Downstream estimation}
\State Feed $\tilde{\boldsymbol{\mu}}_s$ into CausalForestDML (honest splitting)
\State $\mathrm{se}_{\mathrm{adj},i} \gets \sqrt{\mathrm{diag}(\hat{C}_{S_i}^{-1}\, \mathrm{diag}(\mathrm{se}_i^2)\, \hat{C}_{S_i}^{-\top})}$
\end{algorithmic}
\end{algorithm}

\begin{figure*}[t]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=\textwidth]{figures/fig4_budget_compact.pdf}
\caption{RMSE vs.\ expert budget under extreme heterogeneity. Crossover at $n{=}250$ ($K{=}2$) and $n{=}1000$ ($K{=}4$, $\Delta\tau{=}0.3$); under the Table~\ref{tab:main_results} DGP, $K{=}4$ crossover is lower ($n{\approx}500$). Shaded bands: $\pm 1$ SD. Full grid in Appendix~\ref{app:budget}.}
\label{fig:budget_compact}
\end{figure*}

\subsection{Hierarchical Bayes Confusion Matrix Estimation}

We place a Dirichlet-Multinomial hierarchical model over $\{C_s\}_{s=1}^K$, estimating the full $J \times J$ matrix per subgroup (no symmetric-error assumption).
Each row $j$ of $C_s$ has prior $\text{Dir}(\alpha_0 \cdot \hat{C}_{\text{global},j})$, where $\alpha_0 > 0$ is a shared concentration parameter estimated via empirical Bayes (marginal likelihood maximization; $<$5ms; MCMC alternative in Appendix~\ref{app:mcmc}).
We place a $\mathrm{Gamma}(3, 1)$ hyperprior on $\alpha_0$, robust to $2\times$ perturbation (Appendix~\ref{app:hyperprior}).
Large $\alpha_0$ pulls estimates toward $\hat{C}_{\text{global}}$; small $\alpha_0$ allows subgroup-specific rates to dominate.
The key design choice is hierarchical partial pooling rather than independent per-subgroup estimation: when expert labels are scarce ($n_{\text{expert}} / K < 100$), per-subgroup MLE suffers high variance from small cell counts, while global pooling discards the heterogeneity that Theorem~\ref{thm:bias_identity} identifies as the bias source.
HB outperforms stratified MLE in 87.5\% of configurations (Section~\ref{sec:ec_hte_eval}).

\subsection{Subgroup-Stratified Correction}

Given posterior mean confusion matrices $\hat{C}_s$, we correct observed outcome moments per subgroup by matrix inversion:
\begin{equation}
\tilde{\boldsymbol{\mu}}_s = \hat{C}_s^{-1} \cdot \hat{\boldsymbol{\mu}}_s,
\end{equation}
where $\hat{\boldsymbol{\mu}}_s = [\hat{\tau}_{\text{obs}}(0, s),\, \hat{\tau}_{\text{obs}}(1, s)]^\top$ collects the observed conditional outcome moments in subgroup $s$.
Invertibility requires $\pi_s \neq 0.5$ (A4); Laplace smoothing and a $\kappa$-guard ($\kappa(\hat{C}_s) > 100 \to$ fallback to $\bar{C}^{-1}$; threshold ablation in Appendix~\ref{app:kappa_ablation}) ensure finite-sample stability.
All 11 tested LLM--dataset combinations fall in $\kappa \in (1.25, 3]$ with coverage $\geq$93\% (Appendix~\ref{app:kappa_analysis}).
Global correction applies $\bar{C}^{-1}$ uniformly, introducing the $\delta_s \cdot \Delta\tau$ bias of Theorem~\ref{thm:bias_identity}; subgroup-stratified correction eliminates this at the cost of higher variance (Appendix~\ref{sec:budget}).

\subsection{Downstream Integration}

Corrected covariates are fed into CausalForestDML~\cite{wager2018causal} with honest splitting; bias corrections propagate as weighted averages of per-subgroup corrections ($R^2 > 0.97$ across 520 configurations; Appendix~\ref{app:bootstrap_se}).
Standard errors are adjusted via $\text{se}_{\text{corrected},i} = \sqrt{\text{diag}(\hat{C}_{S_i}^{-1}\, \text{diag}(\text{se}_i^2)\, \hat{C}_{S_i}^{-\top})}$; below $n_{\text{expert}}/K < 50$, a bootstrap ($B{=}100$) maintains $\geq$95\% coverage (Appendix~\ref{app:bootstrap_se}).

\paragraph{PPCI composition.}
EC-HTE corrects covariate misclassification; PPCI~\cite{sui2026ppci} corrects outcome-side error. Under both error types, composition reduces $|\text{bias}|$ from 0.179 to 0.007 (Appendix~\ref{sec:ppci}).

\section{Experiments}

\subsection{Setup}

We test on synthetic ($N{=}5{,}000$, $K \in \{2, 4\}$, controlled $\Delta\tau$ and $\Delta\pi$, 100 Monte Carlo (MC) runs) and semi-synthetic data-generating processes (DGPs) spanning four NLP tasks: sentiment analysis (TweetEval~\cite{barbieri2020tweeteval}, $K{=}4$), hate speech detection (CivilComments, $K{=}4$), stance detection ($K{=}5$; Appendix~\ref{app:stance}), and topic classification (AG News, $K{=}4$; Appendix~\ref{app:agnews}), with full specifications in Appendix~\ref{app:dgp}.

We compare: (1) \textit{Naive} (no correction), (2) \textit{Global} (pooled confusion matrix, equivalent to PPI/DSL~\cite{angelopoulos2023ppi,egami2023dsl} with homogeneous error), (3) \textit{Stratified MLE} (per-subgroup DSL without shrinkage), (4) \textit{HB EC-HTE} (per-subgroup correction), (5) \textit{PPCI}~\cite{sui2026ppci}, and (6) \textit{EC-HTE+PPCI}.
For CausalForest~\cite{wager2018causal}, we compare naive, global, and EC-HTE variants.
Primary metrics are $|\text{bias}|$, root mean squared error (RMSE), and 95\% confidence interval (CI) coverage, averaged across subgroups and 100 MC runs.
Robustness validations cover eight conditions including asymmetric errors, subgroup misspecification, and treatment-dependent misclassification (all $\geq$89.5\% coverage; Appendices~\ref{app:hatespeech}--\ref{app:treatment_dep}).
Semi-synthetic evaluation uses empirical confusion matrices from 11 LLM--dataset combinations (eight LLMs across two datasets; Appendix~\ref{app:realcm_bias}; semi-real CM validation in Appendix~\ref{app:semireal}; open-source LLM validation in Appendix~\ref{app:multilm_opensource}).

\subsection{Empirical Validation of the Bias Identity}
\label{sec:bias_mechanism}

\begin{table}[t]
\centering
\caption{Results under moderate heterogeneity and semi-synthetic data. Moderate: $\Delta\pi \leq 0.10$; TweetEval: empirical subgroup structure ($K{=}4$). Metrics averaged over subgroups, $z$-levels, 100 MC runs. $^\dagger$RMSE exceeds Global.}
\label{tab:main_supp}
\small
\resizebox{\columnwidth}{!}{%
\begin{tabular}{ll ccc}
\toprule
Configuration & Method & $|\text{Bias}|$ & RMSE & Cov. \\
\midrule
\multirow{4}{*}{\shortstack[l]{Synthetic DIM\\$K{=}2$, moderate, $n{=}250$}}
 & Naive & 0.018 & 0.064 & 0.95 \\
 & Global & 0.009 & 0.074 & 0.96 \\
 & Strat.\ MLE & \textbf{0.005} & 0.075$^\dagger$ & 0.97 \\
 & HB EC-HTE & \textbf{0.005} & 0.075$^\dagger$ & 0.97 \\
\midrule
\multirow{4}{*}{\shortstack[l]{Semi-synthetic\\TweetEval, extreme, $n{=}250$}}
 & Naive & 0.027 & 0.103 & 0.93 \\
 & Global & \textbf{0.023} & 0.124 & 0.92 \\
 & Strat.\ MLE & 0.032 & 0.248$^\dagger$ & 0.91 \\
 & HB EC-HTE & 0.027 & 0.225$^\dagger$ & 0.91 \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}}
\end{table}

We verify Theorem~\ref{thm:bias_identity} on a $K{=}2$ grid (520 configurations, $\Delta\pi \in [0, 0.25]$, $\Delta\tau \in (0, 0.50]$) and a $K{=}4$ grid (Appendix~\ref{app:dgp}).
Regressing observed subgroup bias on $\delta_s \cdot \Delta\tau / (1{-}2\bar{\pi})$ yields $R^2 = 0.994$ ($K{=}2$) and $0.97$ ($K{=}4$); residual variance reflects MC noise ($R^2 \geq 0.976$ even at $P(Z{=}1) = 0.1$; Appendix~\ref{app:classimbalance}).
Global correction worsens bias in 50\% of $K{=}2$ configurations (260/520, excluding $\Delta\pi{=}0$), with amplification reaching 2.86$\times$ ($K{=}2$) and 2.41$\times$ ($K{=}4$); Figure~\ref{fig:heatmap} visualizes the amplification surface.
Global correction also reverses the sign of estimated CATE in 14.9\% of configurations at $\tau(0) = 0.03$, with all eight real LLMs amplifying bias; EC-HTE eliminates sign reversal (Figure~\ref{fig:sign_reversal}; Appendix~\ref{app:policy_reversal}).
Cross-task validation on stance detection ($K{=}5$, Appendix~\ref{app:stance}) confirms sign reversal (13.3\% $\to$ 3.3\%) and reveals limitations when FNR${}>0.50$.

\paragraph{Real-world case study.}
On CivilComments (Appendix~\ref{app:civilcomments}), GPT-4.1 per-subgroup misclassification rates range from 0.266 to 0.383 ($\Delta\pi = 0.117$).
EC-HTE reveals a 3.2$\times$ effect-size gap between identity subgroups that global correction masks, consistent with known annotation disparities~\cite{borkan2019nuanced}.
At FNR $= 53.3\%$, however, the CM approaches singularity, confirming the boundary condition: EC-HTE needs both sufficient labels \emph{and} $\text{FNR} < 0.50$.

\begin{figure}[t]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=\columnwidth]{figures/fig_sign_reversal_C.pdf}
\caption{Sign reversal rate increases as true CATE $\tau(0) \to 0$: at $\tau(0) = 0.03$, 14.9\% of configurations exhibit sign reversal; all 8 real LLMs amplify bias; EC-HTE eliminates reversal in the $K{=}2$ setting.}
\label{fig:sign_reversal_rate}
\end{figure}

\subsection{Correction Evaluation}
\label{sec:ec_hte_eval}

Table~\ref{tab:main_results} presents the main results.
In the $K{=}2$ extreme row, global correction \emph{increases} $|\text{bias}|$ relative to naive (0.059 vs.\ 0.058), confirming Theorem~\ref{thm:bias_identity}; F-L corrections fare worse (0.122--0.168).
HB EC-HTE reduces $|\text{bias}|$ from 0.059 to 0.008 (7.4$\times$); relative to stratified MLE (0.012), the advantage is 1.5$\times$ with lower RMSE from partial pooling. At $K{=}4$, bias reduces from 0.056 to 0.012 (4.7$\times$), maintaining near-nominal coverage (0.94--0.96).
Under moderate heterogeneity ($\Delta\pi \leq 0.10$), methods converge: HB EC-HTE and global correction yield indistinguishable $|\text{bias}|$ (0.005 vs.\ 0.009) and RMSE (0.075 vs.\ 0.074), confirming the diagnostic's guidance (Table~\ref{tab:main_supp}).
On semi-synthetic TweetEval ($K{=}4$, empirical subgroup structure), EC-HTE RMSE (0.225) exceeds global (0.124) because the empirical $\Delta\pi$ (${\approx}0.10$) falls below the recommended threshold; practitioners who apply the screening criterion correctly would retain global correction here (Table~\ref{tab:main_supp}).
EC-HTE requires $n_{\text{expert}} \geq 250$ ($K{=}2$) or $\geq 500$ ($K{=}4$); below this, RMSE exceeds global (Figure~\ref{fig:budget_compact}).
At $K{=}8$ with $n_{\text{expert}}{=}250$ (${\approx}31$ labels per subgroup), HB partial pooling reduces stratified MLE RMSE by 47\% but still exceeds global correction, confirming $K \leq 4$ as the practical scalability boundary (Appendix~\ref{app:k8_subgroups}).

\paragraph{CausalForest.}
CausalForest~\cite{wager2018causal} with a noisy $\hat{Z}$ inherits the same $M_{\text{global}}^{-1} M_s$ structure.
EC-HTE reduces $|\text{bias}|$ from 0.191 to 0.039 (4.9$\times$) with coverage 0.90 ($K{=}4$, $n{=}500$; oracle coverage decomposition in Appendix~\ref{app:cf_oracle}); R-learner yields comparable reduction (89\%, Appendix~\ref{app:rlearner}), confirming the bias arises from the confusion matrix structure, not the downstream estimator.

\paragraph{PPCI composition.}
Under joint covariate and outcome misclassification, EC-HTE+PPCI reduces $|\text{bias}|$ from 0.179 to 0.007, with coverage $\geq$94\% (Appendix~\ref{sec:ppci}).

\begin{table}[t]
\centering
\caption{Main results (extreme heterogeneity). Metrics averaged over subgroups, $z$-levels, 100 MC runs. Bold: lowest $|\text{bias}|$. $^\dagger$RMSE exceeds Global. F-L Scalar/Per-sub.: \citet{frazis2003estimating}. Moderate and semi-synthetic results in Table~\ref{tab:main_supp}; MC SEs $\leq 0.005$ (Appendix~\ref{app:mcse}).\protect\footnotemark}
\label{tab:main_results}
\resizebox{\columnwidth}{!}{%
\begin{tabular}{ll ccc}
\toprule
Configuration & Method & $|\text{Bias}|$ & RMSE & 95\% CI Cov. \\
\midrule
\multirow{6}{*}{\shortstack[l]{DIM\\$K{=}2$, $n{=}250$}}
 & Naive & 0.058 & 0.090 & 0.82 \\
 & Global & 0.059 & 0.099 & 0.87 \\
 & F-L Scalar & 0.122 & 0.159 & 0.68 \\
 & F-L Per-sub. & 0.168 & 0.220 & 0.65 \\
 & Strat.\ MLE & 0.012 & 0.094 & 0.95 \\
 & HB EC-HTE & \textbf{0.008} & 0.089 & 0.94 \\
\midrule
\multirow{4}{*}{\shortstack[l]{DIM\\$K{=}4$, $n{=}500$}}
 & Naive & 0.062 & 0.113 & 0.88 \\
 & Global & 0.056 & 0.125 & 0.91 \\
 & Strat.\ MLE & \textbf{0.010} & 0.127$^\dagger$ & 0.95 \\
 & HB EC-HTE & 0.012 & 0.120 & 0.96 \\
\midrule
\multirow{3}{*}{\shortstack[l]{CausalForest\\$K{=}4$, $n{=}500$}}
 & Naive & 0.208 & 0.231 & 0.44 \\
 & Global & 0.191 & 0.244 & 0.58 \\
 & EC-HTE Forest & \textbf{0.039} & 0.224 & 0.90 \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}}
\footnotetext{CausalForest plug-in SE achieves 93.8\% aggregate coverage; a bootstrap procedure ($B{=}100$) recovers $\geq$95\% coverage at all budgets tested (Appendix~\ref{app:bootstrap_se}).}
\end{table}

\subsection{Diagnostic and Operating Conditions}
\label{sec:diagnostic}

Theorem~\ref{thm:bias_identity} operates on the full matrix residual $B_s = M_{\text{global}}^{-1} M_s - I$; the scalar diagnostic below is its (A2)-simplified form.
Proposition~\ref{prop:debiasing_hurts} provides a $\Delta\tau$-free diagnostic: global correction increases bias in subgroup $s$ iff $|\delta_s| / ((1-2\bar{\pi}) \cdot \pi_s) > 1$, computable from a pilot sample without estimating treatment effects.
The diagnostic achieves subgroup-level AUC $= 0.86$ under symmetric misclassification and $0.96$ under asymmetric misclassification across 48 FPR/FNR configurations (Figure~\ref{fig:diagnostic_roc}).
In practice, the cost of a false negative (failing to detect bias amplification) exceeds that of a false positive (applying EC-HTE unnecessarily), favoring a recall-oriented threshold.
Under relaxed screening, 79\% of real LLM--dataset pairs show RMSE improvement with coverage $\geq$93\% (Appendix~\ref{app:screening}).

\begin{figure*}[t]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=\textwidth]{figures/fig4_roc.png}
\caption{Per-subgroup diagnostic AUC under symmetric (0.86) and asymmetric (0.96) misclassification across 48 FPR/FNR configurations. The diagnostic correctly identifies all harmed subgroups across 8 semi-real LLM confusion matrices.}
\label{fig:diagnostic_roc}
\end{figure*}

\paragraph{Operating conditions.}
EC-HTE requires: (i)~$\Delta\pi > 0.15$, (ii)~$n_{\text{expert}} \geq 250$ for $K{=}2$, (iii)~FNR${}<0.50$, (iv)~$K \leq 4$, and (v)~$n_{\text{expert}} \geq 500$ under asymmetric errors (FPR$\neq$FNR); when unmet, global correction is preferable (Appendix~\ref{app:k8_subgroups}).
Robustness checks (noisy labels, confounded treatment assignment (Appendix~\ref{app:confounded_dgp}), joint assumption violations) appear in Appendices~\ref{app:robustness}--\ref{app:multilm}.
Across 11 real LLM--dataset combinations (eight LLMs: five proprietary and three open-source; Appendix~\ref{app:multilm_opensource}), the bias identity achieves aggregate $R^2 = 0.975$ (per-model range 0.76--0.99), and EC-HTE improves RMSE over global correction in 79\% of screening configurations with coverage $\geq$93\%.

